{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"competition","sourceId":144271,"databundleVersionId":17462760}],"dockerImageVersionId":31400,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Heavy Equipment Selling Price Prediction Challenge\n\n##  Introduction\n\nWelcome to this analysis and modeling notebook! This project explores a rich, comprehensive dataset detailing the operational,<br>\ntransactional, and technical lifecycles of heavy industrial machinery tracked by a global platform.\n\nEach record within this dataset represents a single, finalized accounting or operational event. It is a highly multi-modal dataset that includes:\n* **System Metadata & Geographic Indicators** (e.g., region codes, transaction dates)\n* **Deep Component Specifications** (e.g., drivetrain type, cabin configurations)\n* **Mechanical Configurations & Usage Metrics** (e.g., operational hours, asset age)\n\n##  Project Objective\n\nOur primary goal is to conduct a thorough Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) to uncover latent patterns hidden across numerical,<br>categorical, and high-cardinality text features. Utilizing these insights, we will engineer meaningful features and train robust machine learning pipelines<br> to accurately forecast the target financial/operational variable (sale price).\n\ntarget to perdict: **TargetValue**\n\n## Evaluation Metric\n\nModel performance and final submissions will be evaluated using the **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)**.\n\nThis metric ensures that we are penalized proportionately for relative errors rather than absolute errors, which is critical when dealing with highly skewed financial targets.\n\n---\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 1. Setup & Load Data","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import warnings\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n\nimport os\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Tell pandas to format floats to 2 decimal places instead of scientific notation\npd.set_option('display.float_format', lambda x: '%.2f' % x)\n\n# Visualization\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n\n\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler,OrdinalEncoder,OneHotEncoder\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import KFold,train_test_split,RandomizedSearchCV,StratifiedKFold\nfrom sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\n\n\n# models\nimport lightgbm as lgb\nimport xgboost as xgb\nfrom catboost import CatBoostRegressor\nfrom sklearn.neural_network import MLPRegressor\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor, ExtraTreesRegressor\n\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error\nfrom sklearn.metrics import root_mean_squared_error,r2_score\n\n\nsns.set_theme(style='whitegrid', palette='muted', font_scale=1.1)\n\nprint(f\"libaries imported \")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\ntrain_path='/kaggle/input/competitions/heavy-equipment-selling-price-prediction-challenge/train.csv'\ntest_path='/kaggle/input/competitions/heavy-equipment-selling-price-prediction-challenge/test.csv'\n\ntrain=pd.read_csv(train_path, low_memory=False)\n\ntest=pd.read_csv(test_path, low_memory=False)\n\nprint(f\"data loaded!\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n#### Parser Stabilization (`low_memory=False`)\nBecause the raw data files mix distinct conceptual categories (strings, missing values, integers, and symbolic placeholders) heavily across columns, pandas' default chunk-based parsing engine is highly prone to casting mismatch conflicts. \n* Enforcing `low_memory=False` mandates an all-at-once memory sweep over the 138,701 rows to accurately infer data types globally.\n* This eliminates downstream computational fragility and silences latent execution warnings (`DtypeWarning`) at a negligible operational RAM cost (~53 MB).\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 2. EDA (Exploratory Data Analysis)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2.1 Data Types Analysis & Descriptive Statistics ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"train.sample(5)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(train.shape)\nprint(train.info())","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(test.shape)\nprint(test.info())","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"Summary count by dtype:\")\nprint(\"train data\")\nprint(train.dtypes.value_counts())\nprint(\"test data\")\nprint(train.dtypes.value_counts())\n\nnum_cols = train.select_dtypes(include=[np.number]).columns.tolist()\ndesc_stats = train[num_cols].describe().T\ndesc_stats['skew'] = train[num_cols].skew()\n\n\nprint(\"\\nDescriptive Statistics of Numerical Variables of train dataset :\")\nprint(desc_stats.to_string())\n\n\nTARGET = 'TargetValue'\nID_COL = 'TransactionID'","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n* **`TargetValue` (Skew = 0.97):** This right-skewed distribution shows a mean of `$41,521.87` , which is pulled well above the median of `$35,000.00` by expensive equipment outliers. Because our optimization metric is **RMSLE** (Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error), training on raw values would cause standard squared loss quadratic penalties to over-index on large errors. Applying a log-transformation (`y_log = np.log1p(TargetValue)`) is required to linearize the target scale and stabilize prediction variance.\n* **`ManufactureYear` (Min = 1001, Skew = -2.55):** A manufacturing year of 1001 is a severe data-entry anomaly acting as a default system placeholder. Leaving it native distorts any age-based engineered features (e.g., `MachineAge = TransactionYear - ManufactureYear`). These extreme values must be replaced with `NaN` and handled via a proper imputation strategy (like median replacement) before training.\n* **`OperationalHoursMeter` (Max = 1,729,600, Skew = 33.28):** An operational log of 1.7 million hours is physically impossible for heavy machinery. This extreme value creates a massive skewness of 33.28. Furthermore, the 25th percentile being 0 indicates a heavy zero-inflation. We must clip or winsorize this feature at a realistic operational ceiling (e.g., 30,000–50,000 hours) to prevent gradient-based trees from creating highly biased splits.\n* **`ID and Nominal Variables`:** While `TransactionID` and `ProductConfigID` register as numerical types (`int64`), they are structurally ID-based and categorical. `TransactionID` must be dropped from training to prevent data leakage, and `ProductConfigID` should be treated via target-encoding or embedding pipelines rather than continuous numeric transformations.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2.2 Missing Values & Outlier Profiling","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"miss_series = (train.isnull().mean() * 100).sort_values(ascending=False)\nprint(\"Missing Value % :\")\nprint(miss_series)\n\nprint(f\"\\nDuplicate rows: {train.duplicated().sum()}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n### Matrix Completeness & Sparsity Map\nAn evaluation of feature completeness reveals a highly sparse matrix, with several columns exhibiting extreme missingness thresholds. Out of 50 features, over 30 contain missing data, which can be categorized into clear tiers of degradation:\n\n* **Extreme Sparsity Tier (>90% Missing):** `col18` and `col19` are missing **99.95%** of their values, meaning they possess fewer than 70 concrete rows. Similarly, `col4` and `col5` are missing **92.52%** of their values.\n* **Severe Sparsity Tier (50% to 90% Missing):** A large cluster of anonymized specification attributes (e.g., `col27` through `col15`), as well as domains like `Forks` and `DrivetrainType`, cross the 60%+ missingness mark.\n* **Moderate Sparsity Tier (20% to 50% Missing):** Critical mechanical usage metrics, such as `OperationalHoursMeter` (**40.84%** missing), fall into this zone.\n* **Zero Duplication Rate:** The dataset registers exactly **0 duplicate rows**, confirming that despite high internal sparsity, every record represents a unique transactional instance.\n\n---\n\n### Downstream Modeling Influence & Tactical Strategy\n\n1. **Strategic Feature Pruning (>90% Threshold):** Features exceeding 90% missingness (`col18`, `col19`, `col4`, `col5`) contain virtually zero statistical signal. Attempting to impute these columns would introduce massive synthetic noise and induce overfitting. These 4 features will be dropped entirely from the final training matrix.\n\n2. **Dual-Tier Numerical Imputation (`OperationalHoursMeter`):** Missing values in mechanical meters are rarely random; they often indicate unrecorded usage or older equipment. \n   * To capture this structural pattern, we will generate a binary indicator column: `Hours_isna = OperationalHoursMeter.isnull().astype(int)`.\n   * The original column will then be imputed using a robust central tendency metric (**Median**), which prevents the extreme right-hand outliers (e.g., the 1.7M hours anomaly) from biasing the imputed values.\n\n3. **Categorical Sentinel Encoding (`UtilizationTier`, `Spec_ReleaseSeries`, etc.):** Rather than forcing a mode imputation that artificially inflates the frequency of the majority class, all missing values in categorical columns will be mapped to a dedicated string sentinel like `'Missing'` or `'Unknown'`. Non-linear tree-based models (`LightGBM`, `CatBoost`) treat this as a distinct, valid structural state, allowing them to map specific risk or price profiles to unrecorded features natively.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(f\"unique items in train:\")\nprint(train.nunique())","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n#### 1. Primary Identifiers & Target (The Core)\n* **`TransactionID`** *(138,701 unique)*: The unique receipt or auction ID for that specific sale. Because it has exactly 138,701 unique values, this matches the total row count. It is simply an index and should be **dropped** before training the model.\n* **`TargetValue`** *(675 unique)*: This is what the models are trying to predict. In this domain, it is almost certainly the Sale Price or Auction Value of the machine.\n* **`AssetID`** *(120,111 unique)*: The unique ID of the specific machine. Notice this number is smaller than `TransactionID`? That means some machines were sold, used, and then resold at auction more than once!\n\n#### 2. Machine Specs & Physical Properties\n*These features define exactly what kind of machine is being sold.*\n* **`ProductConfigID`**: A code representing the specific configuration/model setup of the machine.\n* **`ManufactureYear`**: The year the machine was built. *(Note: Watch out for the `1000`/`1001` outlier we found earlier!)*\n* **`DrivetrainType`** *(8 unique)*: How power moves to the wheels/tracks (e.g., Four-Wheel Drive, Two-Wheel Drive, Track-type).\n* **`CabinType`** *(3 unique)*: The operator's environment (e.g., Open Canopy, Enclosed Cab with A/C). This heavily impacts price!\n* **`Forks`** *(2 unique)*: Indicates if the machine has a forklift attachment setup (True/False or Yes/No).\n* **`Spec_BaseClass`** & **`Spec_SubClass`**: The category of machine (e.g., \"Excavator\", \"Track Dozer\", \"Skid Steer\").\n\n#### 3. Usage & Condition (The \"Odometer\")\n*Just like a car's mileage, these tell  how hard the machine was worked.*\n* **`OperationalHoursMeter`** *(14,643 unique)*: The machine's odometer. It measures how many total hours the engine has run. High hours usually translate to a lower price.\n* **`UtilizationTier`** *(3 unique)*: A simplified rating of how heavily the asset was used (e.g., \"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"). An `OrdinalEncoder` is perfect for this column.\n\n#### 4. Sales & Logistics Data\n* **`TransactionDate`** *(3,676 unique)*: The exact day the machine was sold. <br>\n  *Crucial Step: Do not feed this raw text date into your model. Extract features from it like `SaleYear`, `SaleMonth`, and `Age_At_Sale` (which is `SaleYear - ManufactureYear`).*\n* **`RegionCode`** *(53 unique)*: The geographic area or state where the auction took place (likely US states + a few Canadian provinces). Prices vary depending on local industrial demand!\n* **`VendorPartnerID`** *(31 unique)*: The dealer or auction house broker managing the sale.\n\n#### 5. The Anonymized Columns (`col1` to `col30`)\nColumns like `col20`, `col21`, etc., have been intentionally renamed or masked by the provider to protect proprietary business secrets. However, based on their values, we can decode what some of them represent:\n* **Tracks & Shoes**: Columns containing \"Steel\" or \"Rubber\" are likely tracking types (e.g., Track Width or Shoe Type).\n* **Attachment Sizes**: Columns containing \"9' 6\\\"\" or \"20 inch\" are attachment sizes (e.g., Bucket width, Blade size).\n* **Configuration Types**: Columns containing \"Triple\" or \"Double\" refer to the Mast Type on a forklift or the grouser type on an excavator track.\n\n> ** Handling NaNs in Anonymized Columns:**\n> \n> Notice the high frequency of `NaN` values in `col20` through `col30`. These features only apply to specific types of machines (e.g., a bulldozer doesn't have a forklift mast).  `SimpleImputer` will likely need to treat these missing values as a distinct category, such as `\"None\"` or `\"Missing\"`.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with pd.option_context('display.max_rows', None):\n    print(train['ManufactureYear'].value_counts())","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"test.nunique()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with pd.option_context('display.max_rows', None):\n    print(test['ManufactureYear'].value_counts())","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 3. Visualizations (10 Plots with Insights)\nWe now display distinct plots to visually explore univariate and bivariate patterns, as well as data missingness.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1,2,figsize=(14, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.1: TargetValue Distribution (Raw vs. Log-Transformed)',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\n\n# Raw distribution(histogram plot)\n\naxes[0].hist(train[TARGET],bins=80,color='steelblue', edgecolor='white',alpha=0.85)\naxes[0].axvline(train[TARGET].mean(),color='red',ls='--',lw=2,label=f\"Mean: ${train[TARGET].mean():,.0f}\")\naxes[0].axvline(train[TARGET].median(),color='lime',ls='--',lw=2,label=f\"Median: ${train[TARGET].median():,.0f}\")\naxes[0].set_title(f\"Raw Price Distribution (Skew:{train[TARGET].skew():.2f})\")\naxes[0].set_xlabel(\"Sale Price ($)\")\naxes[0].legend()\n\n# Log-transformed distribution\nlog_target = np.log1p(train[TARGET])\naxes[1].hist(log_target,bins=80,color='darkorange',edgecolor='white',alpha=0.85)\naxes[1].set_title(f\"Log-Transformed Price (Skew:{log_target.skew():.2f})\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"Log(Price + 1)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Plot 3.1: Target Transformation Analysis\nThe target feature (`TargetValue`) displays an initial right-skewed profile (**Skew: 0.97**), driven by high-value industrial asset outliers that stretch the distribution out to $140,000. \n\n* **The Variance Squashing Mechanism:** Applying a mathematical log-transformation ($\\text{log}(x+1)$) compresses the variance scale dynamically. Because the logarithmic derivative decreases monotonically ($d/dx(\\ln x) = 1/x$), larger domain scales are heavily compressed relative to smaller entry margins.\n  \n* **Gaussian Alignment:** This process shifts the feature space toward a symmetric Gaussian curve (**Skew: -0.20**), moving the overall dataset closer to standard normality criteria required for optimal linear-model stability and minimized structural loss errors.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n# Plot 3.2: Missingness rate\nmiss_df=miss_series[miss_series>0].rename('missing_%').to_frame()\nfig, ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.2: Missing Value Rate by Feature',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\ncolors_m = ['#e74c3c' if v> 90 else '#e67e22' if v> 50 else '#3498db' for v in miss_df['missing_%']]\nax.bar(miss_df.index, miss_df['missing_%'],color=colors_m, edgecolor='white')\nax.axhline(90, color='red', ls='--', lw=1.5,label='>90% Missing')\nax.axhline(50, color='orange', ls='--',lw=1.5,label='>50% Missing')\nax.set_ylabel(\"Missing %\")\nax.tick_params(axis='x',rotation=70,labelsize=8)\nax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n\n# Plot 3.3: Map of Missing Value\nplt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\n# cbar=False turns off the color scale legend; yticklabels=False hides row numbers\nsns.heatmap(train.isnull(),cbar=False,yticklabels=False,cmap='viridis')\nplt.title('Plot 3.3:Visual Map of Missing Values (Yellow = Missing)', fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nplt.xticks(rotation=70, fontsize=9)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n\n\n### Plot 3.2: Missingness Multi-Tier Map\nThe feature completeness landscape can be systematically partitioned into three distinct risk tiers based on operational structural integrity thresholds:\n\n* <span style=\"color:#e74c3c\">**Red Zone (>90% Sparsity):**</span> Attributes `col19`, `col18`, `col5`, and `col4` possess near-zero informational density. Keeping these variables forces models to rely on highly synthetic imputations, injecting severe artificial noise and triggering prediction overfitting. They are flagged for automatic column dropping.\n* <span style=\"color:#e67e22\">**Orange Zone (50% - 90% Sparsity):**</span> Contains specific heavy equipment indicators (e.g., `Forks`, `DrivetrainType`, `UtilizationTier`) along with an extensive string of anonymized tracking variables. These columns require robust sentinel missingness encoding (`'Unknown'`) to preserve pattern structures cleanly within gradient boosted tree branches.\n* <span style=\"color:#3498db\">**Blue Zone (<50% Sparsity):**</span> Contains key continuous metrics like `OperationalHoursMeter` (**40.84%** missing). This specific tier is best handled through joint strategic preprocessing: a binary missingness indicator column (`Hours_isna`) combined with a robust median imputation to protect underlying distribution models from tail distortions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.4: Median Price by Equipment Classification\ncat_price=(train.groupby('FunctionalClassification')[TARGET]\n             .agg(['median','count'])\n             .query('count >= 0')\n             .sort_values('median')\n             .tail(15))\n\nfig,ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.4: Median Price by Equipment Classification most 15 ',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nax.barh(cat_price.index,cat_price['median'], color=sns.color_palette('plasma',len(cat_price)))\nax.set_xlabel(\"Median TargetValue ($)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# Plot 3.4: Median Price by Equipment Classification\ncat_price=(train.groupby('FunctionalClassification')[TARGET]\n             .agg(['median','count'])\n             .query('count >= 0')\n             .sort_values('median')\n             .head(15))\n\nfig,ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.4: Median Price by Equipment Classification last 15',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nax.barh(cat_price.index,cat_price['median'], color=sns.color_palette('plasma',len(cat_price)))\nax.set_xlabel(\"Median TargetValue ($)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Plot 3.4: Value Hierarchies by Equipment Classification\nAggregating asset prices across high-volume classifications (minimum 200 trades) highlights distinct pricing thresholds determined by functional capacity and heavy equipment dimensions:\n* **The High-End Tier:** Large industrial machinery dominates the maximum distribution peaks. `Motorgrader - 170.0 to 200.0 Horsepower` exhibits the highest valuation threshold with a median asset cost approaching `$91,000`, closely followed by heavy-duty earth-moving assets (`Wheel Loader - 200.0 to 225.0 Horsepower & Track Type Tractor, Dozer - 260.0 + Horsepower`) hovering near `$68,000`.\n* **The Base-Load Tier:** Large tracked excavators (`Hydraulic Excavator, Track - 21.0 to 33.0 Metric Tons`) compose a predictable baseline price anchor between **$45,000 and $50,000**.\n* **Engineering Impact:** Standard ordinal label encoding would obscure these numeric step-changes. Because explicit performance capacities (Horsepower, Metric Tons) map monotonically to higher price valuations, incorporating **Target Encoding** or utilizing string parsing to extract physical integers from `FunctionalClassification` will yield highly predictive numeric features.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"                                     \n# Plot 3.5: Manufacture Year Anomaly\n\nfig,axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.5: Manufacture Year Anomaly Detection',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\naxes[0].hist(train['ManufactureYear'],bins=80,color='steelblue',edgecolor='white')\naxes[0].axvline(1001,color='red',lw=2,ls='--',label=f\"Placeholder 1001:{(train['ManufactureYear']==1001).sum():,}rows\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Raw Year Distribution (includes 1001)\")\naxes[0].legend()\n\nvalid_years = train[train['ManufactureYear']>1920]['ManufactureYear']\naxes[1].hist(valid_years,bins=60,color='seagreen',edgecolor='white')\naxes[1].set_title(\"Filtered Year Distribution (>1920)\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"Year of Manufacture\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n### Plot 3.5: Temporal Anomaly & Systemic Outlier Detection\nAnalyzing the temporal feature space exposes a major systemic data-logging placeholder that would break standard linear assumptions or distance calculations if left raw:\n\n* **The 1001 Chronological Anomaly:** The raw timeline contains a severe, artificial spike at the year 1001, encompassing exactly **14,719 rows** (roughly **10.6%** of the entire training matrix). This is a legacy system placeholder representing missing or corrupted historical data entry records.\n* **True Production Lifecycle Distribution:** Filtering out the system default reveals a valid manufacturing lifecycle distribution spanning from 1965 to 2015. Asset trade volume accelerates intensely through the late 1990s, peaking around the year **2000**, with a secondary cyclical spike around **2006** before declining due to natural industry economic variations.\n* **Engineering Impact:** Treating `1001` as a continuous numeric integer distorts distance parameters and weight distributions. We must convert these values to `NaN` globally. Post-conversion, an ideal feature engineering approach involves calculating a clean equipment lifespan feature: `MachineAgeAtTransaction = TransactionYear - ManufactureYear`, utilizing a robust median imputation strategy for the newly introduced missing cells.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.6: Region Code Boxplot\n\ntop_regions=train['RegionCode'].value_counts().head(30).index  # index give name instead of value\nregion_data=[train[train['RegionCode']==r][TARGET].values for r in top_regions]\nmedian_prices=[np.median(d) for d in region_data]\norder=np.argsort(median_prices)[::-1]\n\nfig,ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.6: Sale Price Distribution across Top 30 Regions', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nbp = ax.boxplot([region_data[i] for i in order], labels=[top_regions[i] for i in order], \n                patch_artist=True, medianprops=dict(color='black', lw=2),\n                flierprops=dict(marker='.', ms=2, alpha=0.3))\ncolors = sns.color_palette('Set2', len(top_regions))\nfor patch, c in zip(bp['boxes'], colors):\n    patch.set_facecolor(c)\n    patch.set_alpha(0.7)\nax.set_ylabel(\"Sale Price ($)\")\nax.tick_params(axis='x', rotation=25)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Geographic Variations & Feature Interaction Profiling\n\n### Plot 3.6: Spatial Price Distribution Across Elite Regions\nAnalyzing the target asset valuation across the top eight highest-volume trading regions reveals geographic shifts in transaction profiles:\n* **The High-Value Southeast Market:** `Georgia`, `Florida`, and `Texas` exhibit the highest regional valuation structures, establishing a consistent baseline median price near `$40,000`. The larger interquartile ranges (the height of the boxes) in these states indicate a highly diverse trading catalog ranging from smaller support tools to premier production assets.\n* \n* **The Low-Value Northwest Coast:** `Washington` displays a significantly compressed market footprint with a lower median trading point near `$28,000` and noticeably truncated lower percentiles.\n* **The Outlier Stream:** Every single region contains a dense, continuous line of high-value outliers reaching up to **$140,000**. This pattern confirms that regardless of geography, premium specialized equipment retains a high flat value.\n* **Engineering Impact:** The subtle step-changes in the medians and shifting variance distributions across regions prove that location provides clear geographic value signals. Grouping low-frequency regions together while implementing **Target Encoding** or high-cardinality **Frequency Encoding** for major hubs will allow the model to catch regional market premiums cleanly.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n# Plot 3.7: Operational Hours vs Sale Price\nsample_df=train[train['OperationalHoursMeter']<=25000].dropna(subset=['OperationalHoursMeter']).sample(20000,random_state=42)\n\nfig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.7: Operational Hours vs. Sale Price', fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nax.scatter(sample_df['OperationalHoursMeter'],sample_df[TARGET],alpha=0.05, s=3, color='royalblue')\nslope,intercept=np.polyfit(sample_df['OperationalHoursMeter'],sample_df[TARGET], 1)\nx_vals=np.linspace(0, 25000, 200)\nax.plot(x_vals,slope*x_vals+intercept, color='red', lw=2, label=f'Trend Slope: {slope:.3f}')\nax.set_xlabel(\"Operational Hours\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"Sale Price ($)\")\nax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# It comes down to overplotting, extreme outliers, and computer performance. that why used sample instead of whole data ","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n---\n\n### Plot 3.7: Non-Linearity & Bivariate Feature Disconnect\nA scatter plot correlating equipment usage hours (capped at a realistic operational ceiling of 25,000 hours to exclude corrupted inputs) against final sale prices reveals a major modeling hurdle:\n\n* **The Zero-Inflation Spike:** A massive vertical column of data points is concentrated directly on the **0 operational hours** mark, stretching across the entire price span ($7,500 to $140,000). This confirms widespread usage indicator missingness, where fully functional equipment was entered with a zeroed meter by default.\n* **Counter-Intuitive Trend Line:** The linear regression trend line exhibits a flat, slightly positive slope (**0.338**). In asset evaluation, more runtime hours should mathematically decrease valuation due to physical depreciation. The flat positive trend line occurs because the raw signal is heavily distorted by raw size factors—larger, inherently expensive equipment accumulates more total run hours before resale compared to light tools.\n* **Engineering Impact:** A standard linear framework will completely misinterpret this relationship. To fix this interaction trap, tree models require multi-variable interaction support. We must engineer structural interaction features such as **Hours-to-Age Ratio** (`OperationalHoursMeter / MachineAge`) and pass the binary missingness indicator flag (`Hours_isna`) created earlier to help tree splits separate true zero-hour equipment from unrecorded entries.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.8: Pearson Heatmap\ntmp = train.copy()\ntmp['SaleYear']=pd.to_datetime(tmp['TransactionDate'],errors='coerce').dt.year  # coerce this convert to nan of missing data insted of crashing \ntmp['EquipAge']=tmp['SaleYear'] - tmp['ManufactureYear'].where(tmp['ManufactureYear']>1920, np.nan)\ntmp['LogHours']=np.log1p(tmp['OperationalHoursMeter'].clip(upper=50000).fillna(0))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.8: Pearson Correlation Heatmap',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nsns.heatmap(tmp[[TARGET,'EquipAge', 'LogHours', 'SaleYear']].corr(), \n            annot=True,fmt='.3f', cmap='coolwarm', center=0, linewidths=0.5, ax=ax)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Linear Associations & Categorical Operational Demarcation\n\n### Plot 3.8: Linear Correlation Structural Breakdown\nThe Pearson correlation matrix evaluates structural multi-collinearity and isolated linear signal strengths across our engineered numeric feature domain:\n* **The Dominant Negative Vector (`EquipAge`):** Out of all numerical features mapped, `EquipAge` yields the strongest standalone baseline linear association with our target (**-0.352**). This confirms that physical chronological depreciation serves as a fundamental mathematical driver of asset devaluation.\n* **The LogHours Disconnect:** The correlation between the engineered log-transformed runtime variable (`LogHours`) and the target drops to an absolute **-0.000**. While it seems counter-intuitive that usage metric hours show zero linear connection to value, this occurs due to heavy interaction confounding: larger, premium-tier machinery runs for thousands of hours while maintaining high residual values, masking the localized depreciation signal.\n* **Collinearity Observation:** A moderate positive baseline correlation exists between `LogHours` and `SaleYear` (**0.395**). This highlights temporal shifts in data collection—newer transactions are systematically recording operational hours more consistently than legacy ones.\n* **Engineering Impact:** Linear models (like Ridge Regression) will struggle to use `LogHours` effectively due to this zero-correlation mask. However, non-linear ensemble algorithms will successfully unpack this feature by splitting it down branch steps in combination with structural asset dimensions.\n\n---\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.9: Utilization Tier vs Price\ntier_order = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\ntier_data = [train[train['UtilizationTier'] == t][TARGET].dropna().values for t in tier_order]\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 4))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.9: Sale Price across Utilization Tiers', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nbp = ax.boxplot(tier_data, labels=tier_order, patch_artist=True,\n                medianprops=dict(color='black', lw=2),\n                flierprops=dict(marker='.', ms=2, alpha=0.3))\ncolors = ['#27ae60', '#e67e22', '#e74c3c']\nfor patch, c in zip(bp['boxes'], colors):\n    patch.set_facecolor(c)\n    patch.set_alpha(0.7)\nax.set_ylabel(\"TargetValue ($)\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n### Plot 3.9: Value Stratification Across Utilization Tiers\nEvaluating how final trade valuations map across explicit mechanical `UtilizationTier` classifications outlines a clear, monotonic progression:\n* **The Low Utilization Tier:** Assets categorised under a `Low` operating volume profile map to a lower median market position near `$24,000`, with a tightly packed upper quartile bounding near $40,000.\n\n  \n* **The High Utilization Tier:** Equipment classified as `High` utilization commands a distinctly superior median price point near **$47,000**, shifting the entire interquartile range upward.\n* **The Classification Paradox:** In automotive markets, higher utilization typically implies heavier wear, driving prices *down*. In industrial heavy equipment, however, a \"High\" utilization label indicates premium, heavy-duty production assets (e.g., massive continuous-use mining excavators) that naturally carry significantly larger capital valuations than light support equipment.\n* **Engineering Impact:** The distinct, non-overlapping upward shift in medians proves that `UtilizationTier` provides a powerful predictive sorting signal. Since the category ordering directly maps to an increasing price structure, implementing **Ordinal Encoding** (`Low: 0, Medium: 1, High: 2`) will allow your model to capture this natural value progression perfectly.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.10: Yearly Volume vs. Price Trend\n\nannual_data=train.groupby(tmp['SaleYear'])[TARGET].agg(['median', 'count']).reset_index()\nannual_data=annual_data.rename(columns={'SaleYear': 'Year'})\nfig, ax1=plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.10: Yearly Auction Volume vs. Median Price',fontsize=14,fontweight='bold')\nax2=ax1.twinx()\nax1.bar(annual_data['Year'],annual_data['count'],color='lightsteelblue',alpha=0.5, label='Volume')\nax2.plot(annual_data['Year'],annual_data['median'], 'o-',color='darkorange',lw=2.5, label='Median Price')\nax1.set_ylabel(\"Transaction Volume\")\nax2.set_ylabel(\"Median Sale Price ($)\")\nax1.set_xlabel(\"Year of Transaction\")\nh1,l1=ax1.get_legend_handles_labels()\nh2,l2=ax2.get_legend_handles_labels()\nax1.legend(h1+h2,l1+l2, loc='upper left')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Macroeconomic Trends & Missingness Structural Co-occurrence\n\n### Plot 3.10: Temporal Volatility & Economic Market Drivers\nEvaluating transactional volume overlaid with median asset pricing across a twenty-year horizon reveals a clear macroeconomic correlation driven by external market shocks:\n* **The 2008 Financial Crisis Impact:** From 1995 to 2007, trading volumes grew slowly while median prices rose to a historical peak of `$40,000 `in 2007. Following the global financial crisis, the heavy equipment market experienced a dramatic structural shift.People where using used one to save money.\n* **The 2010 Supply Shock:** By the year 2010, auction volume exploded to its absolute peak (over 21,000 transactions). This spike represents a massive market liquidation event where liquidating firms flooded the secondary market with assets. Due to this extreme oversupply coupled with an economic recession, median selling prices collapsed to their absolute lowest channel bottom at **$31,500**. people again started new one .\n  \n* **Engineering Impact:** Time-based variables carry massive cyclical macroeconomic shifts. Because asset values drop during recessions regardless of physical quality, treating `TransactionDate` as a simple counter will fail. We must extract temporal components to create explicit macroeconomic indicators, such as **Yearly Market Liquidation Volume** and a **Recession Indicator Flag** (e.g., mapping the 2008–2011 downturn window) to adjust baseline value expectations.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot 3.11: Missingness heatmap correlation pattern\n\nmoderate_miss_cols=miss_df[miss_df['missing_%'].between(20, 80)].index.tolist()[:12]\nfig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 5))\nfig.suptitle('Plot 3.11: Missingness Correlation Pattern (Sample 2000 rows)', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nsns.heatmap(train[moderate_miss_cols].head(2000).isnull(),cbar=False,yticklabels=False,cmap='YlOrRd',ax=ax)\nax.set_xticklabels(ax.get_xticklabels(),rotation=45,ha='right',fontsize=9)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n\n### Plot 3.11: Structural Missingness & Systemic Co-occurrence Maps\nA null-value matrix visualization isolating attributes with moderate missingness thresholds (20% to 80%) reveals that data missingness is not completely random (MNAR - Missing Not At Random):\n* **The Categorical Block-Null Pattern:** The matrix shows thick horizontal red blocks matching exactly across parallel feature subsets. For instance, when an entry is missing `col20`, it is simultaneously missing `col25` and `col21`. \n* **The Spec Collection Pipeline Break:** This pattern proves that missingness is dictated by structural collection failures during inventory entry. If an asset belongs to a specific configuration tier or is handled by a particular vendor partner, a whole block of descriptive characteristics is skipped by default.\n* **The Completeness Anchor (`UtilizationTier`):** In contrast, the sparse light-yellow bands in `UtilizationTier` indicate that this specific field operates independently of the other columns' missingness patterns.\n* **Engineering Impact:** This strong co-occurrence confirms that dropping features based on single column counts can destroy valuable group signals. It is highly recommended to create a unified missingness flag profile, such as a **Missingness Vector Hash** or an aggregated row-wise metric like `Total_Missing_Specs = df[spec_cols].isnull().sum(axis=1)`. This allows tree algorithms to use the missingness pattern itself as a powerful predictive feature.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 4. Feature Engineering & valdination","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.1 Feature Engineering","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 4.1 FEATURE ENGINEERING\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"Applying advanced feature engineering on raw data...\")\n\ndef engineer_features(df_in):\n    df = df_in.copy()\n    # Parse dates\n    df['TransactionDate'] = pd.to_datetime(df['TransactionDate'], errors='coerce')\n    df['Trans_Year'] = df['TransactionDate'].dt.year\n    df['Trans_Month'] = df['TransactionDate'].dt.month\n    df['Trans_DayOfWeek'] = df['TransactionDate'].dt.dayofweek\n\n    # Handle Year Anomaly (replace 1001 placeholder with NaN)\n    df['ManufactureYear_is_placeholder'] = (df['ManufactureYear'] == 1001).astype(int)\n    df.loc[df['ManufactureYear'] == 1001, 'ManufactureYear'] = np.nan\n    df['MachineAge'] = (df['Trans_Year'] - df['ManufactureYear']).clip(lower=0)\n\n    # Operational Hours Winsorization & log scaling\n    df['OperationalHoursMeter'] = df['OperationalHoursMeter'].clip(upper=60000)\n    df['LogHours'] = np.log1p(df['OperationalHoursMeter'])\n\n    # Usage Intensity\n    df['Usage_Intensity'] = df['OperationalHoursMeter'] / (df['MachineAge'] + 1)\n    df['LogUsage_Intensity'] = np.log1p(df['Usage_Intensity'])\n\n    return df\n\n# Transform raw datasets (assumes 'train' and 'test' are already loaded)\ntrain_eng = engineer_features(train)\ntest_eng  = engineer_features(test)\n\ny_log = np.log1p(train_eng['TargetValue'])\ndrop_cols = ['TransactionID', 'AssetID', 'TransactionDate', 'TargetValue', 'col18', 'col19', 'col4', 'col5']\nfeatures_raw = [col for col in train_eng.columns if col not in drop_cols]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-07-22T06:16:44.345337Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-07-22T06:16:44.345669Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-07-22T06:16:44.364117Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-07-22T06:16:44.345641Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-07-22T06:16:44.362796Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Applying advanced feature engineering on raw data...\n","output_type":"stream"},{"traceback":["\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m","\u001b[0;31mNameError\u001b[0m                                 Traceback (most recent call last)","\u001b[0;32m/tmp/ipykernel_943/3902940140.py\u001b[0m in \u001b[0;36m<cell line: 0>\u001b[0;34m()\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     28\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     29\u001b[0m \u001b[0;31m# Transform raw datasets (assumes 'train' and 'test' are already loaded)\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0;32m---> 30\u001b[0;31m \u001b[0mtrain_eng\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0mengineer_features\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m(\u001b[0m\u001b[0mtrain\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m)\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1;32m     31\u001b[0m \u001b[0mtest_eng\u001b[0m  \u001b[0;34m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0mengineer_features\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m(\u001b[0m\u001b[0mtest\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m)\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     32\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n","\u001b[0;31mNameError\u001b[0m: name 'train' is not defined"],"ename":"NameError","evalue":"name 'train' is not defined","output_type":"error"}],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### 4.1.1 Temporal Feature Extraction\n* **`Trans_Year`**, **`Trans_Month`**, **`Trans_DayOfWeek`**: Extracted from `TransactionDate` to account for macroeconomic trends, annual inflation, and monthly auction volume variations.\n* **`MachineAge`**: Calculated as `Trans_Year - ManufactureYear`. Asset age at transaction time serves as the primary physical driver of value depreciation.\n* **`ManufactureYear_is_placeholder`**: Binary flag indicating records where `ManufactureYear == 1001` prior to median imputation.\n\n#### 4.1.2 Usage Metrics & Non-Linear Intensity Features\n* **`OperationalHoursMeter` (Winsorized)**: Capped at a realistic ceiling of 60,000 hours to eliminate physically impossible values and extreme gradient distortions.\n* **`LogHours`**: Log-transformed meter hours ($\\ln(1 + x)$) to compress right-skewed variance and stabilize linear relationships.\n* **`Usage_Intensity`**: Computed as $\\frac{\\text{OperationalHoursMeter}}{\\text{MachineAge} + 1}$. Measures how aggressively an asset was operated per year of service.\n* **`LogUsage_Intensity`**: Log-transformed usage intensity metric.\n\n#### 4.1.3 Categorical Handling & Column Pruning\n* **Ultra-Sparse Feature Dropping**: Columns missing $>90\\%$ of values (`col18`, `col19`, `col4`, `col5`) are dropped entirely to prevent synthetic imputation noise.\n* **Non-Predictive Column Exclusion**: `TransactionID`, `AssetID`, `TransactionDate`, and `TargetValue` are excluded from model training features.\n* **Sentinel Category Mapping**: Unrecorded string values in categorical features are mapped to a dedicated `'Missing'` sentinel class.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.2 Validation Strategy — Chronological Split","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n# =========================================================================\n# 4.2.1 RANDOM TRAIN/VAL SPLIT (80/20)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"Creating Random Train/Val Splits (80/20)...\")\n\n# Random 80/20 split on raw features and log target\nX_train_native, X_val_native, y_train, y_val = train_test_split(\n    train_eng[features_raw], y_log, test_size=0.2, random_state=42\n)\nX_test_native = test_eng[features_raw].copy()\n\n\n'''\n# =========================================================================\n# 4.2.2 CHRONOLOGICAL TRAIN/VAL SPLIT (80/20)\n# =========================================================================\n\n# Sort by TransactionDate to get the correct chronological order\nsort_idx = train_eng['TransactionDate'].sort_values().index\nX_sorted = train_eng[features_raw].iloc[sort_idx].copy()\n\ny_sorted = y_log.iloc[sort_idx].copy()\n\nsplit_idx = int(len(X_sorted) * 0.8)\n\n# Split into trainval (80%) and testval (20%)\nX_train_native = X_sorted.iloc[:split_idx].copy()\nX_val_native = X_sorted.iloc[split_idx:].copy()\n\ny_train = y_sorted.iloc[:split_idx].copy()\ny_val = y_sorted.iloc[split_idx:].copy()\n\nX_test_native = test_eng[features_raw].copy() # Final test set\n\n'''","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Why NOT Random Split?\nThis dataset represents **historical auction events** sorted by `TransactionDate`. \nA random `train_test_split` causes **future data to leak into training**, producing an \nartificially optimistic local RMSLE (~0.22) that does NOT correlate with the Kaggle public score.\n\n### Chronological Split (Correct Approach)\nWe sort all rows by `TransactionDate` and take:\n* **Train**: First 80% of rows (older auctions)\n* **Validation**: Last 20% of rows (most recent auctions)\n\nThis mirrors the competition setup where the test set contains transactions from **after** the training period.\n\n| Split Type | Local RMSLE | Public RMSLE |\n|---|---|---|\n| Random Split | ~0.22 (misleading) | ~ 0.19702|\n| **Chronological Split** | **~0.27** | **~0.19600** |\n\n> The chronological split local score is a reliable proxy for the public leaderboard.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n## 4.3 OOF TRAGET ENCODING WITH SMOOTHING \n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 4.3. TARGET ENCODING (OOF & LEAKAGE-FREE)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"Applying Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding...\")\ntarget_enc_cols = ['FunctionalClassification', 'RegionCode']  # MENTION ABOVE HIGH CARDINADALITY FEATURES (TECHNICAL FEATURES)\nglobal_mean = y_train.mean()\nSMOOTH = 25\n\nfor col in target_enc_cols:\n    X_train_native[f'{col}_te'] = np.nan\n    X_val_native[f'{col}_te'] = np.nan\n    X_test_native[f'{col}_te'] = np.nan\n\n    # 1. K-Fold OOF encoding strictly on X_train_native (80%)\n    kf = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\n    for t_idx, v_idx in kf.split(X_train_native):\n        stats = pd.DataFrame({\n            'cat': X_train_native[col].iloc[t_idx].fillna('missing'),\n            'target': y_train.iloc[t_idx]\n        })\n        agg = stats.groupby('cat')['target'].agg(['mean', 'count'])\n        smoothed_map = (agg['mean'] * agg['count'] + global_mean * SMOOTH) / (agg['count'] + SMOOTH)\n\n        X_train_native.loc[X_train_native.index[v_idx], f'{col}_te'] = X_train_native[col].iloc[v_idx].fillna('missing').map(smoothed_map)\n\n    # 2. Map full  X_val_native averages to testval (20%) and final test\n    stats_full = pd.DataFrame({\n        'cat': X_train_native[col].fillna('missing'),\n        'target': y_train\n    })\n    agg_full = stats_full.groupby('cat')['target'].agg(['mean', 'count'])\n    full_smoothed_map = (agg_full['mean'] * agg_full['count'] + global_mean * SMOOTH) / (agg_full['count'] + SMOOTH)\n\n    X_train_native[f'{col}_te'] = X_train_native[f'{col}_te'].fillna(global_mean)\n    X_val_native[f'{col}_te'] = X_val_native[col].fillna('missing').map(full_smoothed_map).fillna(global_mean)\n    X_test_native[f'{col}_te'] = X_test_native[col].fillna('missing').map(full_smoothed_map).fillna(global_mean)\n\n# Updated features list\nfeatures_updated = X_train_native.columns.tolist()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 4. Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding with Smoothing\n\n#### **Concept & Motivation**\nCategorical features with high cardinality (such as `FunctionalClassification` and `RegionCode`) can create excessively sparse matrices when using standard One-Hot Encoding. **Target Encoding** resolves this by replacing each categorical level with the expected mean of the target variable (`TargetValue`).\n\nHowever, native target encoding introduces two significant risks:\n1. **Data Leakage & Overfitting:** Directly encoding training samples using their own target values leads to target leakage.\n2. **Rare Category Noise:** Categories with very few instances (e.g., 1 or 2 auction records) produce extreme, unrepresentative target averages.\n\n---\n\n#### **Methodology & Mathematical Formulation**\n\nTo eliminate data leakage and stabilize rare category estimates, we combine **K-Fold Out-of-Fold (OOF) Partitioning** with **Empirical Bayes (M-estimate) Smoothing**:\n\n$$\\hat{S}_{\\text{cat}} = \\frac{\\bar{y}_{\\text{cat}} \\cdot N_{\\text{cat}} + \\bar{y}_{\\text{global}} \\cdot m}{N_{\\text{cat}} + m}$$\n\nWhere:\n* $\\bar{y}_{\\text{cat}}$ = Category target mean within the training fold.\n* $N_{\\text{cat}}$ = Total observation count for the category in the fold.\n* $\\bar{y}_{\\text{global}}$ = Global mean target across the full training fold.\n* $m$ = Smoothing weight parameter ($\\text{SMOOTH} = 25$).\n\n---\n\n#### **Pipeline Execution Steps**\n1. **OOF Split:** Divide `X_trainval` into 5 stratified/shuffled folds.\n2. **Validation Encoding:** For each fold, compute the smoothed map strictly on the remaining 4 folds ($80\\%$) and apply it to the holdout fold ($20\\%$).\n3. **Test Mapping:** Compute a global smoothed map over the complete $100\\%$ `X_trainval` distribution and project it onto `X_testval` and `X_test`.\n4. **Fallback Handling:** Any unseen or `NaN` categories are dynamically imputed with the global mean ($\\bar{y}_{\\text{global}}$).\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":" ### 4.3 Target Encoding (Out-of-Fold)\nHigh-cardinality categoricals (`ProductConfigID`, `Spec_BaseClass`, `RegionCode`, `VendorPartnerID`, `FunctionalClassification`) are encoded using **Out-of-Fold Target Encoding** to prevent target leakage:\n* Train fold: 5-Fold OOF mean of `log1p(TargetValue)`\n* Validation/Test: static lookup from training statistics\n\n\n* **The Leakage Prevention Loop:** The training target means are computed purely on isolated internal training splits (`train_idx`), then projected outward onto the validation subset (`val_idx`). This mirrors true inference constraints because the encoding matrix does not integrate information from the data points it is evaluating.\n* **Target Scale Alignment:** The categorical target means are calculated directly against the log-transformed target space (`y_log`). This aligns the newly generated continuous features perfectly with the core model optimization metric (RMSLE).\n* **Global Generalization Fallback:** Unseen categories or missing values within test splits are safely mapped to the overall `y_log.mean()`, ensuring the model pipeline does not introduce missing value states into downstream tree architectures.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.3 Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding with Smoothing\n\n### Out-of-Fold (OOF) Target Encoding Rationale\n\n#### **High-Cardinality Categorical Encoding**\nHigh-cardinality features such as `FunctionalClassification` (hundreds of categories) and `RegionCode` (53 regions) create extremely sparse matrices under standard One-Hot Encoding. Target encoding replaces category strings with target statistics, but doing so directly on training data introduces severe **target leakage**.\n\n#### **5-Fold OOF Scheme with Bayesian Smoothing**\nTo ensure complete leakage prevention:\n1. **Out-of-Fold Split**: Target statistics for each training row are calculated strictly from the remaining 4 folds.\n2. **Bayesian Smoothing ($\\text{SMOOTH} = 25$)**: Small-sample categories are shrunk toward the global log-target mean $y_{\\text{global}}$:\n   $$\\text{TE}(c) = \\frac{n_c \\cdot \\bar{y}_c + m \\cdot y_{\\text{global}}}{n_c + m}$$\n   where $n_c$ is the category count, $\\bar{y}_c$ is the category mean target, and $m = 25$ is the smoothing weight.\n\n> **Key Takeaway**: Target Encoding transforms high-cardinality nominal variables into smooth, continuous conditional expectations while protecting against target leakage.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"##  4.4 Scaling & Imputation","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 4.4. DUAL-TRACK PREPROCESSING (SCALING & ENCODING WITH ALIGNED CATEGORIES)\n# =========================================================================\n\nprint(\"Setting up Preprocessing Tracks...\")\n\n# Track A: Prepare and ALIGN Native Categoricals for LightGBM and XGBoost\n\nfor col in features_updated:\n    if X_train_native[col].dtype == 'object' or not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(X_train_native[col]):\n        # Find union of all categories across Train, Val, and Test\n        train_vals = set(X_train_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        val_vals = set(X_val_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        test_vals = set(X_test_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        all_cats = list(train_vals.union(val_vals).union(test_vals))\n\n        # Convert to pd.Categorical using the exact same categories set\n        X_train_native[col] = pd.Categorical(X_train_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n        X_val_native[col] = pd.Categorical(X_val_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n        X_test_native[col] = pd.Categorical(X_test_native[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n\n\n# Track B: Imputation & Scaling Pipeline for Scikit-learn Baselines\nnum_feats = X_train_native.select_dtypes(include=[np.number]).columns.tolist()\ncat_feats = X_train_native.select_dtypes(include=['category']).columns.tolist()\n\nnum_pipe = Pipeline([\n    ('impute', SimpleImputer(strategy='median')),\n    ('scale',  StandardScaler())\n])\ncat_pipe = Pipeline([\n    ('fill',   SimpleImputer(strategy='constant', fill_value='Unknown')),\n    ('encode', OrdinalEncoder(handle_unknown='use_encoded_value', unknown_value=-1))\n])\n\npreproc = ColumnTransformer([\n    ('num', num_pipe, num_feats),\n    ('cat', cat_pipe, cat_feats)\n], remainder='drop')\n\n# fit_transform on trainval, transform on X_val_native and test\nX_train_proc = preproc.fit_transform(X_train_native)\nX_val_proc = preproc.transform(X_val_native)\nX_test_proc = preproc.transform(X_test_native)\n\nprint(f\"Track A Native Shape: {X_train_native.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Track B Processed Shape: {X_train_proc.shape}\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4.4 Dual-Track Preprocessing Architecture\n\nTo optimize performance across diverse model architectures, we maintain two parallel preprocessing tracks:\n* **Track A (Native Categorical Track)**: Retains pandas categorical types and raw numerical columns for gradient boosted decision trees (`LightGBM` and `XGBoost`).\n* **Track B (Imputed & Scaled Track)**: Applies `SimpleImputer` (median/mode) and `StandardScaler` to generate dense numeric matrices for distance-based, linear, and neural models (`Ridge Regression`, `Random Forest`, `Extra Trees`, `MLP`).\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 5. Model Training\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 5. BASELINE MODELS EVALUATION (TRACK B)\n# =========================================================================\n\nmodels = {\n    'Ridge Regression': Ridge(alpha=10.0),\n    'Random Forest': RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=12, min_samples_leaf=10, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1),\n    'Extra Trees': ExtraTreesRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=12, min_samples_leaf=10, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1),\n    'XGBoost': xgb.XGBRegressor(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=7, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.7, tree_method='hist', random_state=42, verbosity=0),\n    'LightGBM': lgb.LGBMRegressor(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, num_leaves=63, max_depth=8, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.7, random_state=42, verbose=-1, n_jobs=-1),\n\n}\n\nresults = {}\nfor name, model in models.items():\n    print(f\"Training {name}...\")\n    model.fit(X_train_proc, y_train)\n    preds = model.predict(X_val_proc)\n    rmsle_val = root_mean_squared_error(y_val, preds)\n    results[name] = rmsle_val\n    print(f\"  -> Val RMSLE: {rmsle_val:.5f}\")\n\ncomparison_df = pd.DataFrame(list(results.items()), columns=['Model', 'Val_RMSLE']).sort_values('Val_RMSLE')\nprint(\"\\n--- Model Performance Comparison Table ---\")\nprint(comparison_df.to_string(index=False))\n\n# Plotting performance\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 4.5))\nfig.suptitle('Baseline Models Val RMSLE Comparison', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nbars = ax.bar(comparison_df['Model'], comparison_df['Val_RMSLE'], color=sns.color_palette('viridis', len(comparison_df)), edgecolor='black')\nax.set_ylabel(\"Validation RMSLE\")\n\nfor bar in bars:\n    yval = bar.get_height()\n    ax.text(bar.get_x() + bar.get_width()/2, yval + 0.002, f\"{yval:.4f}\", ha='center', va='bottom', fontsize=9)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n\n### Baseline Model Performance Comparison (By Random Train-Test Split)\n\n| Model | Val_RMSLE | Model Family | Key Characteristics |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **XGBoost** | **0.22** | Gradient Boosted Trees | Fast histogram splits & native categoricals |\n| **LightGBM** | **0.22** | Gradient Boosted Trees | Leaf-wise tree growth optimization |\n| **Random Forest** | **0.26** | Bagged Ensemble | Parallel tree bagging |\n| **Extra Trees** | **0.27** | Extremely Randomized Trees | Random threshold selection |\n| **Ridge Regression** | **0.40** | L2 Linear Regression | Linear baseline with regularization |\n\n---\n\n### Baseline Model Performance Comparison (By Chronological Validation Split)\n\n| Model | Val_RMSLE | Temporal Leakage Impact | Generalization Performance |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **LightGBM** | **0.27** | +0.05 | Superior temporal generalization |\n| **XGBoost** | **0.27** | +0.05 | Strong handling of native categoricals |\n| **Extra Trees** | **0.30** | +0.03 | Robust tree-bagging baseline |\n| **Random Forest** | **0.32** | +0.06 | Higher sensitivity to temporal shifts |\n| **Ridge Regression** | **0.42** | +0.02 | High linear bias on non-linear features |\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 6.Hypertunning","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"'''\n\n# =========================================================================\n# 6.1. HYPERPARAMETER TUNING (LIGHTGBM & XGBOOST ONLY VIA GRIDSEARCHCV)\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\n--- Fast Automated Hyperparameter Tuning using GridSearchCV ---\")\n\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import PredefinedSplit, GridSearchCV\n\n# Combine splits for