tween full train and test sets to prevent XGBoostError\nfor col in X_native_full.columns:\n    if X_native_full[col].dtype == 'object' or not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(X_native_full[col]):\n        train_vals = set(X_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        test_vals = set(X_test_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'))\n        all_cats = list(train_vals.union(test_vals))\n\n        # Convert both columns to Categorical sharing the exact same list of categories\n        X_native_full[col] = pd.Categorical(X_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n        X_test_native_full[col] = pd.Categorical(X_test_native_full[col].astype(str).fillna('missing'), categories=all_cats)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 1. LightGBM\nprint(\"Training final LightGBM...\")\nlgb_model = lgb.LGBMRegressor(\n    n_estimators=1200,\n    subsample=0.8,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbose=-1,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_lgb_params\n)\nlgb_model.fit(X_native_full, y_log)\nlgb_test_preds = lgb_model.predict(X_test_native_full)\n\n# Save pure LightGBM submission\nlgb_raw = np.expm1(lgb_test_preds).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': lgb_raw}).to_csv('submission_lgbm.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved submission_lgbm.csv\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 2. XGBoost\nprint(\"Training final XGBoost...\")\nxgb_model = xgb.XGBRegressor(\n    n_estimators=1000,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    tree_method='hist',\n    enable_categorical=True,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbosity=0,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_xgb_params\n)\nxgb_model.fit(X_native_full, y_log)\nxgb_test_preds = xgb_model.predict(X_test_native_full)\n\n# Save pure XGBoost submission\nxgb_raw = np.expm1(xgb_test_preds).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': xgb_raw}).to_csv('submission_xgb.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved submission_xgb.csv\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# =========================================================================\n# 7.2. GENERATING BLENDED SUBMISSIONS\n# =========================================================================\nprint(\"\\n--- Generating Blended Submissions ---\")\n\n# Blend 1: LGBM (50%) + XGBoost (50%)\nprint(\"Creating 50-50 Blend...\")\nblend_5050_log = (0.5 * lgb_test_preds) + (0.5 * xgb_test_preds)\nblend_5050_raw = np.expm1(blend_5050_log).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': blend_5050_raw}).to_csv('submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_50_1.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved: submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_50.csv\")\n\n# Blend 2: LGBM (40%) + XGBoost (60%)\nprint(\"\\nCreating 40-60 Blend (Heavier on XGBoost)...\")\nblend_4060_log = (0.4 * lgb_test_preds) + (0.6 * xgb_test_preds)\nblend_4060_raw = np.expm1(blend_4060_log).clip(0, None)\n#pd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': blend_4060_raw}).to_csv('submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_4060.csv', index=False)\n#print(\"Saved: submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_4060.csv\")\n\n# Blend 3: LGBM (60%) + XGBoost (40%)\nprint(\"\\nCreating 60-40 Blend (Heavier on LightGBM)...\")\nblend_6040_log = (0.6 * lgb_test_preds) + (0.4 * xgb_test_preds)\nblend_6040_raw = np.expm1(blend_6040_log).clip(0, None)\npd.DataFrame({'TransactionID': test['TransactionID'], 'TargetValue': blend_6040_raw}).to_csv('submission', index=False)\nprint(\"Saved: submission_lgbm_xgb_blend_6040.csv\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\n\n'''\nn_estimators=1200,\n    subsample=0.8,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbose=-1,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_lgb_params\n\n\n\nn_estimators=1000,\n    colsample_bytree=0.7,\n    tree_method='hist',\n    enable_categorical=True,\n    random_state=42,\n    verbosity=0,\n    n_jobs=-1,\n    **best_xgb_params\n)\n\n'''\n\n# TRAIN_TEST SPILT with grind cv\n# 0.19748 50-50\n#0.19707   60-40\n# lgbm 0.19805\n# 0.19813 40-60\n\n#TRAIN_TEST SPILT with random cv\n# 0.19740    50-50  \n##0.19704   60-40\n# CRONOGICAL SPILT\n\n#cornogical  with griend cv\n# 0.19708 50-50\n#cornogical  with randomcv \n\n# 0.19729 50-50 \n# 0.19790 60-40 \n\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 🎯 Overfitting vs. Underfitting: Core Concepts & Playbook\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Think of machine learning models like students preparing for an exam:\n> * **Underfitting** is the student who barely studied. They didn't capture the core concepts, so they perform poorly on both practice tests and the final exam.\n> * **Overfitting** is the student who memorized the practice questions word for word. They get a perfect score on the practice tests, but if the real exam changes even a single word, they fail completely because they didn't learn the underlying patterns.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Definitions & The Bias-Variance Tradeoff\n\nTo understand why these occur, we evaluate two foundational forces in Machine Learning: **Bias** (Rigidity) and **Variance** (Flexibility).\n\n### 🐢 Underfitting (High Bias, Low Variance)\n* **What it is:** The model is too simple to capture the underlying structure of the data.\n* **The Cause (High Bias):** The model makes strong, rigid assumptions about the data (e.g., attempting to fit a straight line through data that follows a complex curve).\n* **The Result:** Poor performance on both the training data and new validation data.\n\n### 🐒 Overfitting (Low Bias, High Variance)\n* **What it is:** The model learns both the underlying genuine patterns and the random noise/flukes in the training dataset.\n* **The Cause (High Variance):** The model is overly flexible and hyper-sensitive to small fluctuations in the training set.\n* **The Result:** Near-perfect performance on training data, but poor generalization on new validation data.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Model Sensitivity & Risk Factors\n\n| Condition / Factor | Underfitting Risk (High Bias) | Overfitting Risk (High Variance) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Dataset Size** | N/A | **Small Datasets:** High risk of memorizing individual rows. |\n| **Feature-to-Row Ratio** | N/A | **High Dimensions:** (e.g., 500 columns vs. 1,000 rows) leads to coincidental correlations. |\n| **Model Complexity** | **Oversimplified Models:** Linear models on non-linear spatial data. | **Excessive Complexity:** Deep trees (`max_depth=15`) with 1,000+ estimators. |\n| **Regularization** | **Heavy Constraints:** Penalties set so tight the model cannot learn. | **Unconstrained:** Zero regularization allows uncontrolled growth. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Prevention & Remediation Playbook\n\n[Underfitting / High Bias]  <======  O P T I M A L  ======>  [Overfitting / High Variance]\n    (Model Too Simple)                                           (Model Too Complex)\n\n\n### 🔧 How to Fix Underfitting (Increase Model Capacity)\n1. **Increase Model Complexity:** Give the model more breathing room (e.g., increase `max_depth` or `num_leaves`).\n2. **Feature Engineering:** Add relevant features, interaction terms (`FuncClass_x_InvGroup`), or historical aggregates.\n3. **Reduce Regularization / Train Longer:** Lower L2 penalties (`l2_leaf_reg`) or allow boosting algorithms to run for more iterations (`n_estimators`).\n\n---\n\n### 🛡️ How to Fix Overfitting (Control & Generalize)\n1. **Cross-Validation / Validation Splits:** Use `PredefinedSplit` or K-Fold CV to ensure you never evaluate on data the model has already seen during training.\n2. **Regularization Penalties:** Introduce explicit penalties for structural complexity:\n   * **CatBoost:** `l2_leaf_reg`\n   * **LightGBM / XGBoost:** `reg_alpha` (L1) and `reg_lambda` (L2)\n3. **Subsampling (Feature & Row Masking):**\n   * `subsample=0.7`: Forces each tree to train on a random $70\\%$ subset of rows.\n   * `colsample_bytree=0.6`: Forces each tree to train on a random $60\\%$ subset of features.\n4. **Early Stopping:** Automatically halt training when validation error stops improving, preventing the model from learning training noise.\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 🎛️ Understanding Learning Rate & Tree Balancing\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Learning Rate ($\\eta$ / `learning_rate`):** Controls how aggressively each new tree in Gradient Boosting corrects the mistakes of previous trees.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Comparing Learning Rates\n\n* **High Learning Rate ($\\eta = 0.7$ — Too High):** \n  The first few trees make massive, aggressive adjustments. While it learns quickly, the model overshoots the optimal target, oscillates wildly, and rapidly overfits the training data. It loses the ability to capture subtle, underlying patterns.\n\n* **Optimal Learning Rate ($\\eta = 0.05$ — Just Right):** \n  Each tree contributes a small, controlled amount ($5\\%$) toward the final correction. Subsequent trees gently smooth out remaining residual errors. While it requires more trees, the resulting model is significantly more accurate, robust, and stable.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Balancing Act: Learning Rate vs. `n_estimators`\n\nLearning rate never works in isolation—it operates in direct partnership with `n_estimators` (total tree count). \n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **The Boat Steering Analogy:**\n> * **A $0.7$ Learning Rate** is like yanking the steering wheel hard left and right. You turn fast, but you risk crashing or overcorrecting. You only need a few trees before the model breaks.\n> * **A $0.05$ Learning Rate** is like making small, precise micro-adjustments to the steering wheel. Because each adjustment is small, you need more total turns (`n_estimators = 1000`) to reach your destination safely.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Executive Comparison Matrix\n\n| Feature / Metric | Low Learning Rate (`0.01` – `0.05`) | High Learning Rate (`0.3` – `0.7`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Step Size** | Tiny, incremental steps | Massive, aggressive jumps |\n| **Required Trees (`n_estimators`)** | High (e.g., $800$ to $1500+$ trees) | Very low (e.g., $20$ to $50$ trees) |\n| **Overfitting Risk** | Low (when paired with Early Stopping) | Extremely High |\n| **Final Generalization** | **Excellent** (smooth decision boundaries) | **Poor** (jagged, hyper-specific rules) |\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# MILESTONE 1","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"'''\n# --------------- 1. LOAD DATA --------------------------------\ntrain = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/heavy-equipment-selling-price-prediction-challenge/train.csv', low_memory=False)\ntest  = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/heavy-equipment-selling-price-prediction-challenge/test.csv',  low_memory=False)\n\n\n#---------------questions ----------------\nprint(\"Q-1\")\nprint(train.shape,test.shape)\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint('Q-2')\nprint(set(train.columns) - set(test.columns))\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(train.info())\nprint(\" q-3 there are 6 colums\")\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-4\")\nprint((train.isnull().mean()*100).sort_values(ascending=False))\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-5\")\nprint(train['OperationalHoursMeter'].isnull().mean() * 100)\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-6\")\nprint(train['TargetValue'].median())\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-7\")\nprint(train['ManufactureYear'].unique())\nprint(train['ManufactureYear'].mode()[0])\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-8\")\nprint(train['TransactionDate'].nunique())\nprint(train['TransactionDate'].min())\n\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\n\nprint(\"Q-9\")\nprint(\"Unique classes in Spec_BaseClass:\")\nprint(train['Spec_BaseClass'].nunique())\n\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\nprint(\"Q-10\")\nprint(\"\\nMost frequent region:\")\nprint(train['RegionCode'].mode()[0])\n\nprint(\"*\"*80)\n\n\nprint(\"Q-11\")\n\nflorida_avg = train.loc[\n    train['Regio